stment": 0.16, "key_factors": ["All ten expert ratings call the seat a Democratic flip, ranging from Tilt to Likely D", "Prediction markets price Bennett near 89-90% to win", "Incumbent Kean faces damaging headlines on a stock trade, Trump alignment, and an unexplained House absence", "Bennett has matched Kean's fundraising and holds a large outside-spending edge"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic-leaning environment in an even-PVI district, and every major rater plus the prediction markets treat Kean as the underdog. The lone recent poll (Bennett +5) and the money picture are consistent, so the forecast stays near the combined evidence at roughly D +6.", "watch": "A late shift in the national environment toward Republicans, or a strong debate performance from Kean, could narrow this toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Thomas Kean Jr. is defending an even-PVI New Jersey seat in what looks like a favorable year for Democrats. Challenger Rebecca Bennett has raised over $4 million, nearly matching the incumbent, and outside groups have spent heavily supporting her while largely ignoring Kean. A September live-caller poll showed Bennett up five points among likely voters.\n\nEvery major race rater now ranks the district as a Democratic pickup opportunity, from Tilt D to Likely D, and prediction markets give Bennett roughly a nine-in-ten chance of winning. Kean's vulnerabilities include his alignment with Trump on tariffs and Iran, questions about a stock trade, and scrutiny of an unexplained medical absence from House votes. The race remains competitive on paper, but the weight of the evidence favors Bennett by a moderate margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin": 5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8142, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8286